@node	2.3, 2.4, 2.2, Chapter 2
@section Symbolic Data

All the compound data objects we have used so far were constructed ultimately
from numbers.  In this section we extend the representational capability of our
language by introducing the ability to work with arbitrary symbols as data.

@menu
* 2.3.1::            Quotation
* 2.3.2::            Example: Symbolic Differentiation
* 2.3.3::            Example: Representing Sets
* 2.3.4::            Example: Huffman Encoding Trees
@end menu

@node	2.3.1, 2.3.2, 2.3, 2.3
@subsection Quotation

If we can form compound data using symbols, we can have lists such as

@smallexample
showSymbols(list(sym("a"), sym("b"), sym("c"), sym("d")));
// => "[a, b, c, d]"

showList(list(23, 45, 17));
// => "[23, 45, 17]"

showDatum(listDatum(
  listDatum(symDatum("Norah"), numDatum(12)),
  listDatum(symDatum("Molly"), numDatum(9)),
  listDatum(symDatum("Anna"), numDatum(7)),
  listDatum(symDatum("Lauren"), numDatum(6)),
  listDatum(symDatum("Charlotte"), numDatum(4)),
));
// => "[[Norah, 12], [Molly, 9], [Anna, 7], [Lauren, 6], [Charlotte, 4]]"
@end smallexample

@noindent
All of it is built by plain functions and nothing else: @code{sym("a")} brands
the string @code{"a"} as a @code{Symb}, the edition's symbol type, and
@code{listDatum} glues symbol and number data into one list.  The name/age
table is the first compound value in
this book whose elements mix symbols and numbers; the section's @code{Datum}
type carries exactly that mixture, which is why the nested lists above need no
further machinery.

A list of symbols and numbers can also spell out an expression without
computing it:

@example typescript
showDatum(listDatum(symDatum("+"), symDatum("x"), numDatum(3)));
// => "[+, x, 3]"

showDatum(listDatum(
  symDatum("*"),
  listDatum(symDatum("+"), numDatum(23), numDatum(45)),
  listDatum(symDatum("+"), symDatum("x"), numDatum(9)),
));
// => "[*, [+, 23, 45], [+, x, 9]]"
@end example

@noindent
The first datum describes the sum of @code{x} and 3, the second a product of
two sums.  Each is a value built by ordinary function calls --- calls to
@code{listDatum}, @code{symDatum}, and @code{numDatum}, which answer data ---
and no such call could also be read by the TypeScript compiler as executable
code computing something else.  A language whose programs and data share one
surface spelling needs a marker --- @newterm{quote} --- to say which of the two
a written form is.  TypeScript's syntax for building a value and its syntax
for computing one are different from the start, so the language we work in
never needs such a marker, and the constructors above are the whole of
it.@footnote{The original book marked data forms with a quotation sign that
its reader consumed before evaluation; this edition's constructors are the
same idea made explicit, building the data with ordinary calls and leaving
nothing for a reader to expand.}

This issue --- is a written name the thing itself, or a name standing for the
thing? --- is the distinction between a symbol and the @newterm{values} of the
names that happen to share its spelling, and it is well known in the
context of natural languages, where words and sentences may be regarded either
as semantic entities or as character strings (syntactic entities).  The common
practice in natural languages is to use quotation marks to indicate that a word
or a sentence is to be treated literally as a string of characters.  For
instance, the first letter of ``John'' is clearly ``J.''  If we tell somebody
``say your name aloud,'' we expect to hear that person's name.  However, if we
tell somebody ``say `your name' aloud,'' we expect to hear the words ``your
name.''  Note that we are forced to nest quotation marks to describe what
somebody else might say.@footnote{Allowing quotation in a language wreaks havoc
with the ability to reason about the language in simple terms, because it
destroys the notion that equals can be substituted for equals.  For example,
three is one plus two, but the word ``three'' is not the phrase ``one plus
two.''  Quotation is powerful because it gives us a way to build expressions
that manipulate other expressions (as we will see when we write an interpreter
in @ref{Chapter 4}). But allowing statements in a language that talk about
other statements in that language makes it very difficult to maintain any
coherent principle of what ``equals can be substituted for equals'' should
mean.  For example, if we know that the evening star is the morning star, then
from the statement ``the evening star is Venus'' we can deduce ``the morning
star is Venus.''  However, given that ``John knows that the evening star is
Venus'' we cannot infer that ``John knows that the morning star is Venus.''}

TypeScript never needs the marker that natural language reaches for here,
because a TypeScript value is never ambiguous between ``the thing itself'' and
``a name that stands for the thing.''  @code{list(sym("a"), sym("b"))} builds
a list of exactly two symbols: there is no reading of this call under which
@code{sym("a")} could instead denote the value of some variable named
@code{a}, because @code{sym} always takes a string and always returns a fresh
@code{Symb}.  The confusion a quotation sign solves does not arise, so
there is nothing here for a marker to disambiguate.@footnote{A TypeScript
string literal such as @code{"a"} is a further, unrelated distinction the book
does not need to draw at this point: @code{"a"} is an ordinary string meant to
be printed and compared, and @code{sym("a")} is the one-call conversion that
turns it into the branded, @code{===}-comparable symbol this section
manipulates as data.  The two are never confused, because the edition's symbol
type is a different type, and the compiler rejects the mix.}

Now we can distinguish between symbols and their values:

@example typescript
const a = 1;
const b = 2;

showList(list(a, b));
// => "[1, 2]"

showSymbols(list(sym("a"), sym("b")));
// => "[a, b]"

showDatum(listDatum(symDatum("a"), numDatum(b)));
// => "[a, 2]"
@end example

@noindent
The first call builds a list of the numbers @code{a} and @code{b} are bound
to; the second builds a list of the two symbols; the third mixes them, which
only the section's @code{Datum} union admits.  In keeping with this, the empty
list needs no quotation at all: it is the value @code{nil} of @ref{2.2.1},
and @code{list()} with no arguments builds it too.

One additional primitive used in manipulating symbols is @code{eqQ}, which
takes two data objects and tests whether they are the same symbol
(or the same number).@footnote{We can consider two symbols to be ``the same''
if they consist of the same characters in the same order.  Such a definition
skirts a deep issue that we are not yet ready to address: the meaning of
``sameness'' in a programming language.  We will return to this in
@ref{Chapter 3} (@ref{3.1.3}).}  Using @code{eqQ}, we can implement a useful
procedure called @code{memq}.  This takes two arguments, a datum and a list.
If the datum is not contained in the list (i.e., is not @code{eqQ} to any item
in the list), then @code{memq} returns nothing.  Otherwise, it returns the
sublist of the list beginning with the first occurrence of the datum:

@example typescript
export const memq = (
  item: Datum,
  x: List<Datum>,
): Option<List<Datum>> => @{
  if (x._tag === "Nil") @{
    return none;
  @}
  if (eqQ(item, x.head)) @{
    return some(x);
  @}
  return memq(item, x.tail);
@};
@end example

@noindent
For example, the value of

@example typescript
memq(
  symDatum("apple"),
  list(symDatum("pear"), symDatum("banana"), symDatum("prune")),
);
// => @{ _tag: "None" @}
@end example

@noindent
is nothing, whereas

@example typescript
const fruit = list(
  symDatum("x"),
  listDatum(symDatum("apple"), symDatum("sauce")),
  symDatum("y"),
  symDatum("apple"),
  symDatum("pear"),
);

showDataList(getOrElse(memq(symDatum("apple"), fruit), nil));
// => "[apple, pear]"
@end example

@noindent
renders the sublist @code{[apple, pear]} --- the tail of @code{fruit} that begins
at its first @code{apple}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.53}Exercise 2.53:} @emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 2.53 with a host-language
exercise that keeps its number and teaching objective.}  Predict the value of each of
the following expressions, printed the way this edition prints symbolic data
(@code{showDatum}, @code{showDataList}, and @code{showSymbols}):

@example typescript
showDatum(
  listDatum(symDatum("a"), symDatum("b"), symDatum("c")),
);
showDatum(listDatum(listDatum(symDatum("george"))));

const xy = list(
  listDatum(symDatum("x1"), symDatum("x2")),
  listDatum(symDatum("y1"), symDatum("y2")),
);
showDataList(getOrElse(cdr(xy), nil));
showDatum(
  getOrElse(car(getOrElse(cdr(xy), nil)), numDatum(0)),
);

const headOfA = getOrElse(
  car(
    list(symDatum("a"), symDatum("short"), symDatum("list")),
  ),
  numDatum(0),
);
headOfA._tag === "Lst";

showDataList(
  getOrElse(
    memq(
      symDatum("red"),
      list(
        listDatum(symDatum("red"), symDatum("shoes")),
        listDatum(symDatum("blue"), symDatum("socks")),
      ),
    ),
    nil,
  ),
);
showDataList(
  getOrElse(
    memq(
      symDatum("red"),
      list(
        symDatum("red"),
        symDatum("shoes"),
        symDatum("blue"),
        symDatum("socks"),
      ),
    ),
    nil,
  ),
);
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.54}Exercise 2.54:} Two lists are said to be
@code{equal} if they contain equal elements arranged in the same order.  For
example,

@example typescript
const a = listDatum(
  symDatum("this"),
  symDatum("is"),
  symDatum("a"),
  symDatum("list"),
);
const b = listDatum(
  symDatum("this"),
  symDatum("is"),
  symDatum("a"),
  symDatum("list"),
);
equalQ(a, b);
@end example

@noindent
is true, but

@example typescript
const c = listDatum(
  symDatum("this"),
  listDatum(symDatum("is"), symDatum("a")),
  symDatum("list"),
);
equalQ(a, c);
@end example

@noindent
is false.  To be more precise, we can define the equality recursively in terms
of the basic @code{eqQ} equality of symbols by saying that @code{x} and
@code{y} are @code{equal} if they are both symbols and the symbols are
@code{eqQ}, or if they are both lists such that the head of @code{x} is
@code{equal} to the head of @code{y} and the tail of @code{x} is @code{equal}
to the tail of @code{y}.  Using this idea, implement @code{equalQ} as a
procedure over the section's @code{Datum} union.@footnote{In practice,
programmers use @code{equalQ} to compare lists that contain numbers as well as
symbols.  Numbers are not considered to be symbols.  The question of whether
two numerically equal numbers are also the same piece of data is settled here
by saying yes: @code{eqQ} compares numbers by @code{===}, so a recursive
@code{equalQ} that bottoms out in @code{eqQ} compares numbers numerically as
well.}
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.55}Exercise 2.55:} @emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 2.55 with a host-language
exercise that keeps its number and teaching objective.}  The original's
puzzle is a fact about quotation as data: the value of a doubly quoted word
is a two-item list whose first item is the symbol naming the quotation
operation --- a description of a quotation, not a quotation performed.  This
edition builds that value with one call, since there is no reader to expand
anything:

@example typescript
const valueOfDoubleQuote =
  listDatum(symDatum("quote"), symDatum("abracadabra"));
@end example

@noindent
Predict what @code{showDatum(valueOfDoubleQuote)} renders, and what the first
item of its list renders when @code{car} takes it and @code{showDatum} prints
it; then run both and compare.  Explain which constructor call stands for the
outer quotation and which list item stands for the inner one, and why a datum
may name an operation here while the operation is never performed.
@end quotation

@node	2.3.2, 2.3.3, 2.3.1, 2.3
@subsection Example: Symbolic Differentiation

As an illustration of symbol manipulation and a further illustration of data
abstraction, consider the design of a procedure that performs symbolic
differentiation of algebraic expressions.  We would like the procedure to take
as arguments an algebraic expression and a variable and to return the
derivative of the expression with respect to the variable.  For example, if the
arguments to the procedure are @math{{ax^2 + bx + c}} and @math{x}, the
procedure should return @math{{2ax + b}}.  Symbolic differentiation is of
special historical significance in Lisp.  It was one of the motivating examples
behind the development of a computer language for symbol manipulation.
Furthermore, it marked the beginning of the line of research that led to the
development of powerful systems for symbolic mathematical work, which are
currently being used by a growing number of applied mathematicians and
physicists.

In developing the symbolic-differentiation program, we will follow the same
strategy of data abstraction that we followed in developing the rational-number
system of @ref{2.1.1}.  That is, we will first define a differentiation
algorithm that operates on abstract objects such as ``sums,'' ``products,'' and
``variables'' without worrying about how these are to be represented.  Only
afterward will we address the representation problem.

@subsubheading The differentiation program with abstract data

In order to keep things simple, we will consider a very simple
symbolic-differentiation program that handles expressions that are built up
using only the operations of addition and multiplication with two arguments.
Differentiation of any such expression can be carried out by applying the
following reduction rules:
@displaymath
{{dc \over dx} \,=\, 0,}
  \quad {\hbox{for } c \hbox{ a constant }} {\hbox{or a variable }} {\hbox{different from } x,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
dc
-- = 0  for c a constant, or a variable different from x
dx

dx
-- = 1
dx

d(u + v)   du   dv
-------- = -- + --
   dx      dx   dx

d(uv)     / dv \     / du \
----- = u | -- | + v | -- |
 dx       \ dx /     \ dx /
@end example

@end ifinfo
@displaymath
\eqalign{
{dx \over dx}         &=   1, \cr
{d(u + v) \over dx}   &=   {du \over dx} + {dv \over dx}, \cr
{d(uv) \over dx}      &=   u \kern0.1em {dv \over dx} + v \kern0.1em {du \over dx}.
}
@end displaymath
@noindent
Observe that the latter two rules are recursive in nature.  That is, to obtain
the derivative of a sum we first find the derivatives of the terms and add
them.  Each of the terms may in turn be an expression that needs to be
decomposed.  Decomposing into smaller and smaller pieces will eventually
produce pieces that are either constants or variables, whose derivatives will
be either 0 or 1.

To embody these rules in a procedure we indulge in a little wishful thinking,
as we did in designing the rational-number implementation.  The book assumes
procedures for telling whether an expression is a sum, a product, a constant,
or a variable; for extracting the addend, augend, multiplier, and multiplicand;
and for constructing sums and products from parts.  In this edition, that
assumption is one type: the sum and product constructors are carried
explicitly by the differentiation program, and everything else the book
assumes is a tag read on the expression union defined in the next
subsubsection.  With this, and the primitive check of an expression's
@code{Num} tag, we can express the differentiation rules as the following
procedure:

@example typescript
export const derivVia = (
  exp: Expr,
  v: Symb,
  ctors: SumProductConstructors,
): Expr => @{
  switch (exp._tag) @{
    case "Num":
      return constant(0);
    case "Var":
      return sameVariableQ(exp, v)
        ? constant(1)
        : constant(0);
    case "Sum":
      return ctors.makeSum(
        derivVia(exp.addend, v, ctors),
        derivVia(exp.augend, v, ctors),
      );
    case "Prod":
      return ctors.makeSum(
        ctors.makeProduct(
          exp.multiplier,
          derivVia(exp.multiplicand, v, ctors),
        ),
        ctors.makeProduct(
          derivVia(exp.multiplier, v, ctors),
          exp.multiplicand,
        ),
      );
  @}
@};
@end example

@noindent
This @code{derivVia} procedure incorporates the complete differentiation
algorithm.  Since it is expressed in terms of abstract data, it will work no
matter how we choose to represent algebraic expressions, as long as we design a
proper set of tag checks and constructors.  This is the issue we must address
next.  The original's final catch-all clause, which signals an error for an
unknown expression, is gone for a structural reason: the union has no other
shape, so the switch is exhaustive, and the compiler flags any missing case
instead of waiting for an unknown expression to show up at run time.

@subsubheading Representing algebraic expressions

We can imagine many ways to represent algebraic expressions.  For example, we
could use lists of symbols that mirror the usual algebraic notation, spelling
@math{{ax + b}} as the flat datum @code{[a, *, x, +, b]}.  However, one
especially straightforward choice --- and the one this edition takes --- is to
name each shape with a tag.  Then our data representation for the
differentiation problem is the following union, which replaces the original's
list-structure representation and its nine little procedures: the variable and
number predicates become the @code{Var} and @code{Num} tags; the sum and
product predicates become the @code{Sum} and @code{Prod} tags; the selectors
@code{addend}, @code{augend},
@code{multiplier}, and @code{multiplicand} become the union's fields; and the
constructors are the two function-valued fields of
@code{SumProductConstructors}, which @code{derivVia} takes as an argument so
that the section can swap them without touching the differentiation program:

@example typescript
export type Expr =
  | @{ readonly _tag: "Var"; readonly name: Symb @}
  | @{ readonly _tag: "Num"; readonly n: number @}
  | @{
      readonly _tag: "Sum";
      readonly addend: Expr;
      readonly augend: Expr;
    @}
  | @{
      readonly _tag: "Prod";
      readonly multiplier: Expr;
      readonly multiplicand: Expr;
    @};

export const variable = (name: string): Expr =>
  (@{ _tag: "Var", name: sym(name) @});
export const constant = (n: number): Expr => (@{ _tag: "Num", n @});

export const sameVariableQ = (e: Expr, v: Symb): boolean =>
  e._tag === "Var" && e.name === v;

export interface SumProductConstructors @{
  readonly makeSum: (a1: Expr, a2: Expr) => Expr;
  readonly makeProduct: (m1: Expr, m2: Expr) => Expr;
@}

export const naiveConstructors: SumProductConstructors = @{
  makeSum: (a1, a2) => (@{ _tag: "Sum", addend: a1, augend: a2 @}),
  makeProduct: (m1, m2) =>
    (@{ _tag: "Prod", multiplier: m1, multiplicand: m2 @}),
@};
@end example

@noindent
Two variables are the same if the symbols representing them are @code{===}
equal, which is what @code{sameVariableQ} tests.  Thus, we need only combine
these with the algorithm as embodied by @code{derivVia} in order to have a
working symbolic-differentiation program.  Let us look at some examples of its
behavior, rendering expressions with @code{showExpr} in the usual
mathematical notation:

@example typescript
const x = variable("x");
const y = variable("y");

const sumX3 = naiveConstructors.makeSum(x, constant(3));
const prodXY = naiveConstructors.makeProduct(x, y);
const third = naiveConstructors.makeProduct(prodXY, sumX3);

showExpr(derivVia(sumX3, sym("x"), naiveConstructors));
// => "1 + 0"

showExpr(derivVia(prodXY, sym("x"), naiveConstructors));
// => "x * 0 + 1 * y"

showExpr(derivVia(third, sym("x"), naiveConstructors));
// => "x * y * (1 + 0) + (x * 0 + 1 * y) * (x + 3)"
@end example

@noindent
The program produces answers that are correct; however, they are unsimplified.
It is true that
@displaymath
{d(xy) \over dx} \,=\, {x \cdot 0} + {1 \cdot y,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
d(xy)
----- = x * 0 + 1 * y
 dx
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
but we would like the program to know that @math{{x \cdot 0 = 0}}, @math{{1 \cdot y = y}},
and @math{{0 + y = y}}.  The answer for the second example should have been
simply @code{y}.  As the third example shows, this becomes a serious issue when
the expressions are complex.

Our difficulty is much like the one we encountered with the rational-number
implementation: we haven't reduced answers to simplest form.  To accomplish the
rational-number reduction, we needed to change only the constructors and the
selectors of the implementation.  We can adopt a similar strategy here.  We
won't change @code{derivVia} at all.  Instead, we will change @code{makeSum} so
that if both summands are numbers, @code{makeSum} will add them and return
their sum.  Also, if one of the summands is 0, then @code{makeSum} will return
the other summand:

@example typescript
export const equalsNumber = (e: Expr, n: number): boolean =>
  e._tag === "Num" && e.n === n;

export const makeSum = (a1: Expr, a2: Expr): Expr => @{
  if (equalsNumber(a1, 0)) @{
    return a2;
  @}
  if (equalsNumber(a2, 0)) @{
    return a1;
  @}
  if (a1._tag === "Num" && a2._tag === "Num") @{
    return constant(a1.n + a2.n);
  @}
  return @{ _tag: "Sum", addend: a1, augend: a2 @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
This uses the procedure @code{equalsNumber}, which checks whether an expression
is equal to a given number.  Similarly, we will change @code{makeProduct} to
build in the rules that 0 times anything is 0 and 1 times anything is the thing
itself:

@example typescript
export const makeProduct = (m1: Expr, m2: Expr): Expr => @{
  if (equalsNumber(m1, 0) || equalsNumber(m2, 0)) @{
    return constant(0);
  @}
  if (equalsNumber(m1, 1)) @{
    return m2;
  @}
  if (equalsNumber(m2, 1)) @{
    return m1;
  @}
  if (m1._tag === "Num" && m2._tag === "Num") @{
    return constant(m1.n * m2.n);
  @}
  return @{ _tag: "Prod", multiplier: m1, multiplicand: m2 @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
Here is how this version works on our three examples, with @code{deriv} ---
@code{derivVia} given the simplifying pair --- in place of the naive call:

@example typescript
showExpr(deriv(sumX3, sym("x")));
// => "1"

showExpr(deriv(prodXY, sym("x")));
// => "y"

showExpr(deriv(third, sym("x")));
// => "x * y + y * (x + 3)"
@end example

@noindent
Although this is quite an improvement, the third example shows that there is
still a long way to go before we get a program that puts expressions into a
form that we might agree is ``simplest.''  The problem of algebraic
simplification is complex because, among other reasons, a form that may be
simplest for one purpose may not be for another.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.56}Exercise 2.56:} Show how to extend the basic
differentiator to handle more kinds of expressions.  For instance, implement
the differentiation rule
@displaymath
{d(u^{\kern0.1ex n}) \over dx} \,=\, {nu^{\kern0.1ex n-1} \, {du \over dx}}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
d(u^n)            du
------ = nu^(n-1) --  
  dx              dx
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
by adding a fifth case to the expression union for exponentiation --- a
@code{Pow} tag carrying @code{base} and @code{exponent} fields, rendered
@code{u ** n} --- and an arm to the differentiation switch, together with a
@code{makeExponentiation} constructor that builds the case.  Build in the
rules that anything raised to the power 0 is 1 and anything raised to the
power 1 is the thing itself.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.57}Exercise 2.57:} Extend the differentiation
program to handle sums and products of arbitrary numbers of (two or more)
terms.  Then the last example above could be expressed as

@example typescript
derivN(
    prodN(
      variable("x"),
      variable("y"),
      sumN(variable("x"), constant(3)),
    ),
  sym("x"),
);
@end example

Try to do this by changing only the representation for sums and products,
without changing the shape of the differentiation switch.  For example, the
@code{addend} of a sum would be the first term, and the @code{augend} would be
the sum of the rest of the terms.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.58}Exercise 2.58:} Suppose we want to modify the
differentiation program so that it works with ordinary mathematical notation,
in which @code{+} and @code{*} are infix rather than prefix operators.  Since
the differentiation program is defined in terms of abstract data, we can modify
it to work with different representations of expressions solely by changing the
predicates, selectors, and constructors that define the representation of the
algebraic expressions on which the differentiator is to operate.

@enumerate a

@item
Show how to do this in order to differentiate algebraic expressions presented
in infix form, such as @code{(x + (3 * (x + (y + 2))))}.  In this edition the
expression arrives as quoted data built from the section's constructors,

@example typescript
const infixA = listDatum(
  symDatum("x"),
  symDatum("+"),
  listDatum(
    numDatum(3),
    symDatum("*"),
    listDatum(
      symDatum("x"),
      symDatum("+"),
      listDatum(
        symDatum("y"),
        symDatum("+"),
        numDatum(2),
      ),
    ),
  ),
);
@end example

@noindent
and your program parses it into the section's @code{Expr}.  To simplify the
task, assume that @code{+} and @code{*} always take two arguments and that
expressions are fully parenthesized.

@item
The problem becomes substantially harder if we allow standard algebraic
notation, such as @code{(x + 3 * (x + y + 2))}, which drops unnecessary
parentheses and assumes that multiplication is done before addition.  Can you
design appropriate parsing for this notation such that the differentiation
switch still works?

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@node	2.3.3, 2.3.4, 2.3.2, 2.3
@subsection Example: Representing Sets

In the previous examples we built representations for two kinds of compound
data objects: rational numbers and algebraic expressions.  In one of these
examples we had the choice of simplifying (reducing) the expressions at either
construction time or selection time, but other than that the choice of a
representation for these structures in terms of lists was straightforward. When
we turn to the representation of sets, the choice of a representation is not so
obvious.  Indeed, there are a number of possible representations, and they
differ significantly from one another in several ways.

Informally, a set is simply a collection of distinct objects.  To give a more
precise definition we can employ the method of data abstraction.  That is, we
define ``set'' by specifying the operations that are to be used on sets.  These
are @code{unionSet}, @code{intersectionSet}, @code{elementOfSet}, and
@code{adjoinSet}.  @code{ElementOfSet} is a predicate that determines whether
a given element is a member of a set.  @code{AdjoinSet} takes an object and a
set as arguments and returns a set that contains the elements of the original
set and also the adjoined element.  @code{UnionSet} computes the union of two
sets, which is the set containing each element that appears in either argument.
@code{IntersectionSet} computes the intersection of two sets, which is the set
containing only elements that appear in both arguments.  From the viewpoint of
data abstraction, we are free to design any representation that implements
these operations in a way consistent with the interpretations given
above.@footnote{If we want to be more formal, we can specify ``consistent with
the interpretations given above'' to mean that the operations satisfy a
collection of rules such as these:

@itemize @bullet

@item
For any set @code{S} and any object @code{x},
@code{elementOfSet(x, adjoinSet(x, S))}
is true (informally: ``Adjoining an object to a
set produces a set that contains the object'').

@item
For any sets @code{S} and @code{T} and any object @code{x},
@code{elementOfSet(x, unionSet(S, T))}
is equal to
@code{elementOfSet(x, S) || elementOfSet(x, T)}
(informally: ``The elements of @code{unionSet(S, T)} are the elements that
are in @code{S} or in @code{T}'').

@item
For any object @code{x},
@code{elementOfSet(x, nil)}
is false (informally: ``No object is an element of the empty set'').

@end itemize
}

@subsubheading Sets as unordered lists

One way to represent a set is as a list of its elements in which no element
appears more than once.  The empty set is represented by the empty list.  The
original's unordered membership compares elements with its generic equality
so that they need not be symbols; this edition keeps the section's numeric
examples and compares them with @code{===}, which plays exactly the same role
here (exercise 2.54 develops the structural equality over the section's
@code{Datum} union for the general case):

@example typescript
export const elementOfSetUnordered = (
  x: number,
  set: List<number>,
): boolean => @{
  if (set._tag === "Nil") @{
    return false;
  @}
  if (x === set.head) @{
    return true;
  @}
  return elementOfSetUnordered(x, set.tail);
@};
@end example

@noindent
Using this, we can write @code{adjoinSetUnordered}.  If the object to be
adjoined is already in the set, we just return the set.  Otherwise, we use
@code{cons} to add the object to the list that represents the set:

@example typescript
export const adjoinSetUnordered = (
  x: number,
  set: List<number>,
): List<number> =>
  elementOfSetUnordered(x, set) ? set : cons(x, set);
@end example

@noindent
For @code{intersectionSetUnordered} we can use a recursive strategy.  If we
know how to form the intersection of @code{set2} and the tail of
@code{set1}, we only need to decide whether to include the head of @code{set1}
in this.  But this depends on whether the head of @code{set1} is also in
@code{set2}.  Here is the resulting procedure:

@example typescript
export const intersectionSetUnordered = (
  set1: List<number>,
  set2: List<number>,
): List<number> => @{
  if (set1._tag === "Nil" || set2._tag === "Nil") @{
    return nil;
  @}
  if (elementOfSetUnordered(set1.head, set2)) @{
    return cons(set1.head, intersectionSetUnordered(set1.tail, set2));
  @}
  return intersectionSetUnordered(set1.tail, set2);
@};
@end example

@noindent
In designing a representation, one of the issues we should be concerned with is
efficiency.  Consider the number of steps required by our set operations.
Since they all use @code{elementOfSetUnordered}, the speed of this operation
has a major impact on the efficiency of the set implementation as a whole.
Now, in order to check whether an object is a member of a set,
@code{elementOfSetUnordered} may have to scan the entire set. (In the worst
case, the object turns out not to be in the set.)  Hence, if the set has
@math{n} elements, @code{elementOfSetUnordered} might take up to @math{n}
steps.  Thus, the number of steps required grows as @math{{\Theta(n)}}.  The
number of steps required by @code{adjoinSetUnordered}, which uses this
operation, also grows as @math{{\Theta(n)}}.  For
@code{intersectionSetUnordered}, which does an @code{elementOfSetUnordered}
check for each element of @code{set1}, the number of steps required grows as
the product of the sizes of the sets involved, or @math{{\Theta(n^2)}} for two
sets of size @math{n}.  The same will be true of @code{unionSet}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.59}Exercise 2.59:} Implement the
@code{unionSetUnordered} operation for the unordered-list representation of
sets.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.60}Exercise 2.60:} We specified that a set would be
represented as a list with no duplicates.  Now suppose we allow duplicates.
For instance, the set @math{{\{1, 2, 3\}}} could be represented as the list
@code{[2, 3, 2, 1, 3, 2, 2]}.  Design procedures @code{elementOfSet}, @code{adjoinSet},
@code{unionSet}, and @code{intersectionSet} that operate on this
representation.  How does the efficiency of each compare with the corresponding
procedure for the non-duplicate representation?  Are there applications for
which you would use this representation in preference to the non-duplicate one?
@end quotation

@subsubheading Sets as ordered lists

One way to speed up our set operations is to change the representation so that
the set elements are listed in increasing order.  To do this, we need some way
to compare two objects so that we can say which is bigger.  For example, we
could compare symbols lexicographically, or we could agree on some method for
assigning a unique number to an object and then compare the elements by
comparing the corresponding numbers.  To keep our discussion simple, we will
consider only the case where the set elements are numbers, so that we can
compare elements using @code{>} and @code{<}.  We will represent a set of
numbers by listing its elements in increasing order.  Whereas our first
representation above allowed us to represent the set @math{{\{1, 3, 6, 10\}}} by listing
the elements in any order, our new representation allows only the list
@code{[1, 3, 6, 10]}.

One advantage of ordering shows up in @code{elementOfSetOrdered}: In checking
for the presence of an item, we no longer have to scan the entire set.  If we
reach a set element that is larger than the item we are looking for, then we
know that the item is not in the set:

@example typescript
export const elementOfSetOrdered = (
  x: number,
  set: List<number>,
): boolean => @{
  if (set._tag === "Nil") @{
    return false;
  @}
  if (x === set.head) @{
    return true;
  @}
  return x < set.head ? false : elementOfSetOrdered(x, set.tail);
@};
@end example

How many steps does this save?  In the worst case, the item we are looking for
may be the largest one in the set, so the number of steps is the same as for
the unordered representation.  On the other hand, if we search for items of
many different sizes we can expect that sometimes we will be able to stop
searching at a point near the beginning of the list and that other times we
will still need to examine most of the list.  On the average we should expect
to have to examine about half of the items in the set.  Thus, the average
number of steps required will be about @math{{n / 2}}.  This is still
@math{{\Theta(n)}} growth, but it does save us, on the average, a factor of 2
in number of steps over the previous implementation.

We obtain a more impressive speedup with @code{intersectionSetOrdered}.  In
the unordered representation this operation required @math{{\Theta(n^2)}} steps,
because we performed a complete scan of @code{set2} for each element of
@code{set1}.  But with the ordered representation, we can use a more clever
method.  Begin by comparing the initial elements, @code{x1} and @code{x2}, of
the two sets.  If @code{x1} equals @code{x2}, then that gives an element of the
intersection, and the rest of the intersection is the intersection of the
tails of the two sets.  Suppose, however, that @code{x1} is less than
@code{x2}.  Since @code{x2} is the smallest element in @code{set2}, we can
immediately conclude that @code{x1} cannot appear anywhere in @code{set2} and
hence is not in the intersection.  Hence, the intersection is equal to the
intersection of @code{set2} with the tail of @code{set1}.  Similarly, if
@code{x2} is less than @code{x1}, then the intersection is given by the
intersection of @code{set1} with the tail of @code{set2}.  Here is the
procedure:

@example typescript
export const intersectionSetOrdered = (
  set1: List<number>,
  set2: List<number>,
): List<number> => @{
  if (set1._tag === "Nil" || set2._tag === "Nil") @{
    return nil;
  @}
  const x1 = set1.head;
  const x2 = set2.head;
  if (x1 === x2) @{
    return cons(x1, intersectionSetOrdered(set1.tail, set2.tail));
  @}
  return x1 < x2
    ? intersectionSetOrdered(set1.tail, set2)
    : intersectionSetOrdered(set1, set2.tail);
@};
@end example

@noindent
To estimate the number of steps required by this process, observe that at each
step we reduce the intersection problem to computing intersections of smaller
sets---removing the first element from @code{set1} or @code{set2} or both.
Thus, the number of steps required is at most the sum of the sizes of
@code{set1} and @code{set2}, rather than the product of the sizes as with the
unordered representation.  This is @math{{\Theta(n)}} growth rather than
@math{{\Theta(n^2)}}---a considerable speedup, even for sets of moderate size.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.61}Exercise 2.61:} Give an implementation of
@code{adjoinSetOrdered} using the ordered representation.  By analogy with
@code{elementOfSetOrdered} show how to take advantage of the ordering to
produce a procedure that requires on the average about half as many steps as
with the unordered representation.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.62}Exercise 2.62:} Give a @math{{\Theta(n)}}
implementation of @code{unionSetOrdered} for sets represented as ordered lists.
@end quotation

@subsubheading Sets as binary trees

We can do better than the ordered-list representation by arranging the set
elements in the form of a tree.  Each node of the tree holds one element of the
set, called the ``entry'' at that node, and a link to each of two other
(possibly empty) nodes.  The ``left'' link points to elements smaller than the
one at the node, and the ``right'' link to elements greater than the one at the
node.  @ref{Figure 2.16} shows some trees that represent the set
@math{{\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11\}}}.  The same set may be represented by a tree in a number of
different ways.  The only thing we require for a valid representation is that

